Q fever
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Abrupt high fever/severe headache, Atypical pneumonia, Hepatomegaly, Exposure to livestock/birthing products

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-043
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of extreme fever, excruciating retro-orbital headache, and severe shortness of breath requiring urgent assessment for atypical pneumonia or systemic zoonotic infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an abattoir worker experiencing sudden, overwhelming fever, severe shaking chills, and an inability to breathe deeply without coughing. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate respiratory compromise, rule out acute severe bacterial pneumonia, and initiate specific targeted antibiotic therapy prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm burning up, my head is splitting behind my eyes, and I can't catch a deep breath without coughing dryly."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced abruptly 24 hours ago with high-grade fever, severe shaking chills, and profound drenching sweats. The patient reports an intense, severe generalized headache frequently accompanied by excruciating retro-orbital pain. Extreme generalized myalgia, systemic fatigue, and intense prostration are present. Over the last 12 hours, the patient developed symptoms of an atypical pneumonia, presenting as a dry, non-productive hacking cough and progressive exertional dyspnoea. Complete absence of a distinct cutaneous maculopapular rash. Patient reports a clear history of distinct occupational exposure, working in an abattoir processing sheep and cattle.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly toxic appearance, exhausted by the severe rigors.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is highly anxious given the rapid and severe onset of symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 39.7 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Acutely toxic, diaphoretic, flushed, taking shallow breaths, shading eyes from the light.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals tachypnoea and scattered, diffuse fine bilateral crackles, but no dense focal lobar consolidation or dullness to percussion. Cardiovascular exam reveals severe tachycardia. Abdominal exam reveals mild to moderate tender hepatomegaly (liver edge 2cm below costal margin). Neurological exam shows no frank nuchal rigidity despite the severe headache. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Abrupt high fever/severe headache, Atypical pneumonia, Hepatomegaly, and Exposure to livestock/birthing products.
Environmental Observations: Patient's work boots (potentially contaminated) left near the entry.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. No recent Q fever vaccination history.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Q Fever (Acute phase with atypical pneumonia and hepatitis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Influenza A/B - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of tender hepatomegaly and the highly specific occupational exposure (abattoir worker), though the acute severe febrile prodrome and dry cough are clinically very similar.

Leptospirosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of severe conjunctival suffusion and exquisite calf tenderness, alongside the prominence of the respiratory (pneumonia) symptoms.

Typical Bacterial Pneumonia (Pneumococcal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a dry (not purulent/rusty) cough, lack of dense focal lobar consolidation, and severe retro-orbital headache.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a severe acute febrile illness, atypical pneumonitis, and tender hepatomegaly in the setting of high-risk occupational exposure (abattoir). This triad is highly characteristic of acute Q fever. The presence of significant systemic toxicity, borderline hypoxia (SpO2 94%), and severe tachycardia necessitated an urgent, direct clinical evaluation to formally assess the respiratory reserve, rule out impending respiratory failure, and immediately commence specific tetracycline antibiotics (Doxycycline) which are required to halt this specific intracellular pathogen. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of severe respiratory or hepatic decompensation, fully satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe empiric oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily immediately for 14 days. Request urgent comprehensive blood work: FBC, LFTs (to assess the severity of the granulomatous hepatitis), and highly specific Phase I and Phase II IgM and IgG antibodies to Coxiella burnetii via IFA.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Doxycycline precisely 30 minutes after a large meal with a full glass of water, the patient will prevent severe medication-induced esophagitis and nausea; successfully retaining the dose without severe stomach pain will strongly reinforce strict compliance with the full 14-day course.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory notification to the State Public Health Unit.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 24 hours to monitor oxygen saturations; mandatory in-person review in 48-72 hours to review LFTs and serology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops severe shortness of breath at rest, sharp stabbing chest pain, confusion, a sudden yellowing of the skin/eyes (jaundice), or an absolute inability to keep oral fluids/antibiotics down.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly suspected based on occupational exposure and classic signs; definitive serology is required.
Clinical Course: The Doxycycline will rapidly kill the bacteria. However, severe post-infectious fatigue can persist for months after the acute pneumonia and fever have resolved.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The bacteria have caused inflammation in your lungs and liver. If you find yourself gasping for air while resting in bed, or if your skin suddenly turns yellow, you must call an ambulance immediately as this indicates a severe complication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have contracted a severe bacterial infection (Q fever) from inhaling contaminated dust or particles from the livestock at the abattoir. Standard penicillin-type antibiotics do not work against this bacteria. You must take the specific Doxycycline prescribed to clear the infection from your lungs and liver.
Actionable Steps: Start the Doxycycline immediately. Ensure your work clothes and boots are washed separately in hot water. Rest completely until the fever breaks.

